/// polymorph:visor-spike — the visor's strip and drawer host as a component.
///
/// THE QUESTION THIS SPIKE ASKS: can the framework's trusted UI be a wasm
/// component rendering through `polymorph:dioxus`, instead of ~6.5k lines of
/// TypeScript running as the page's own script? Scope is the STRIP and the
/// DRAWER HOST only (visor/ui/visor.ts); sheets, pairing and entry stay in
/// TypeScript and mount into the drawer as foreign DOM.
///
/// The base world `polymorph:dioxus/app` gives the guest a batched DOM
/// mutation channel and DOM event dispatch. Three things the visor needs are
/// not expressible on it, and each becomes an interface here:
///
///   - `store`   — the visor persists five things; localStorage is a host
///                 capability.
///   - `chrome`  — viewport height, and the measured height of foreign sheet
///                 DOM the guest did not render and therefore cannot address
///                 through `polymorph:dioxus/dom` (which resolves ElementIds
///                 only).
///   - `control` — the visor's own API, re-expressed as data. In TypeScript
///                 it trades in `HTMLElement`; across a component boundary it
///                 cannot, which is the point of running the spike.
///
/// THE TERRITORY. The guest renders the WHOLE of the visor's pixels into its
/// mount root: `#visor-zone` (containing `#visor-drawer` and `#visor-strip`)
/// and `#visor-dim`. Today those elements are skeleton markup in each
/// consumer's index.html (demo/web/index.html:202-230) and `initVisor()`
/// fills them in by id. Moving them here makes the ids one definition rather
/// than one per consumer page — the ids are a trust anchor, and a trust
/// anchor duplicated across consumer HTML is a trust anchor each consumer can
/// get wrong.
package polymorph:visor-spike@0.1.0;

/// The visor's persistence, scoped to the five things it stores.
///
/// NOT a general key/value capability: the guest names a `slot`, and the host
/// maps that to its own consumer-prefixed key (`pm-demo-*`, `pm-todomvc-*` —
/// the keys have always been the consumer's, see visor/README.md). A
/// component that cannot spell a key cannot read the page's other storage,
/// which the TypeScript visor — running as page script with the whole origin's
/// localStorage in reach — has no way to promise.
interface store {
    /// The anchor colour, the audible anchor word, the identity record, the
    /// event record. `legacy-hue` is the pre-rename hue key, read once at
    /// boot and cleared (`loadVisorHue`'s migration).
    /// `marks` is the TRUST TABLE (sheets.ts's `createSurfaceMarks`): the
    /// per-component pet icon, first-sight timestamp and petname. It is a
    /// slot like the rest, so the same scoping holds — the component names
    /// the slot, the host owns the key, and two consumers on one origin do
    /// not share a table.
    /// `account` is the USER-SYSTEM BOOT CACHE: the last-known profile,
    /// device list and account marks, reconciled against the partition at boot
    /// so the difference can be ANNOUNCED rather than silently adopted.
    ///
    /// A SLOT OF ITS OWN, not a corner of `marks`. `marks` is the LOCAL trust
    /// table keyed by provenance; this is account state replicated between the
    /// user's devices. Two different facts sharing one key is exactly the
    /// failure the slot enum exists to prevent, and the pairing wave correctly
    /// refused to reuse one rather than quietly overloading it.
    enum slot { hue, word, identity, events, marks, account, legacy-hue }

    /// `none` = never written (distinct from written-empty).
    get: func(key: slot) -> option<string>;
    set: func(key: slot, value: string);
    remove: func(key: slot);
    /// The erase ceremony: every slot at once, so a slot added later cannot
    /// be forgotten by a caller enumerating them by hand.
    clear-all: func();
}

/// Page-level facts the guest cannot obtain from the mutation surface.
///
/// PROVISIONAL. polyengine is growing opt-in DOCUMENT EVAL, which the visor —
/// being trusted — will be able to use and apps will not. Both functions here
/// exist only because this world cannot otherwise reach the page, so both are
/// candidates to collapse into that capability, and the deleted
/// `measure-sheet` (see below) would become answerable directly rather than
/// needing the host to push heights in-band.
///
/// WHAT MUST NOT MIGRATE ONTO IT: `store`. Its slot enum is the sharpest trust
/// result in this port — the component names a slot, the host owns the key, so
/// a component that cannot spell a key cannot read the origin's other storage.
/// That is a promise the TypeScript visor, running as page script, has no way
/// to make. Reaching localStorage through document eval would hand it straight
/// back. "The visor may eval" and "the visor should eval for everything it
/// can" are different claims, and only the first is implied.
interface chrome {
    /// `globalThis.innerHeight` in CSS pixels — the drawer's height budget is
    /// `innerHeight - strip height - 48`, and only the strip half of that is
    /// a guest-rendered element `polymorph:dioxus/dom` can measure.
    viewport-height: func() -> f64;

    /// Reload the page — `location.reload()`, which the erase ceremony ends
    /// with (sheets.ts:1618-1637).
    ///
    /// WHY THE CEREMONY NEEDS IT, rather than just clearing storage. Erasing
    /// empties the store, but the running instance still holds the identity,
    /// the hue and the event record in memory — so without a reload the visor
    /// goes on saying a name it has just forgotten, and the settings sheet
    /// that resumes underneath re-renders off the stale record. A wipe the
    /// user is told was total must not leave the screen contradicting it.
    ///
    /// It lives on `chrome` for the same reason `viewport-height` does: it is
    /// a property of the page the guest is drawn into, not of the tree the
    /// guest rendered, so the mutation surface cannot reach it.
    reload: func();

    /// WHAT USED TO BE HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT. A `measure-sheet` query, for
    /// the guest to ask the host how tall the foreign sheet DOM is — the
    /// guest never rendered it, so it has no ElementId and
    /// `dom.get-client-rect` cannot see it. The spike built it and then found
    /// it had no caller: the host is the side that mounts the sheet, so the
    /// host is already holding the measurement, and every path that needs the
    /// height gets it pushed in-band through `control.mount-sheet` /
    /// `control.resize-sheet`. A pull query alongside a push that already
    /// carries the value is a second way to know one thing.
    ///
    /// Recorded rather than silently dropped because the reasoning generalises:
    /// on this seam, geometry about foreign DOM flows from whoever owns the
    /// DOM, and the guest never needs to ask.
}

/// The vocabulary `control` (exported) and `embedder` (imported) share.
///
/// A separate interface rather than types defined in `control`, because
/// `use control.{...}` from `embedder` would make the world IMPORT `control`
/// as well as export it — and `control` has functions, so the host would be
/// asked to implement the very interface it calls. A functionless interface
/// imported for its types costs nothing.
interface types {
    /// `SurfaceIdentity`, trimmed to what the strip renders. `meta` and
    /// `first-seen` are sheet-only and stay out of the spike.
    record surface {
        name: string,
        /// APP VOICE. The component's self-declared nickname; rendered only
        /// through the guest's `foreign-token`, which in Rust is a private
        /// constructor rather than a grep (invariant (h) of
        /// demo/scripts/check-invariants.sh).
        nickname: string,
        /// USER VOICE. "" = unmarked, which renders nothing.
        icon: string,
        is-new: bool,
        petname: option<string>,
        /// WHAT THIS COMPONENT ASKED TO WEAR — a glyph the component itself
        /// nominated. PRE-VALIDATED by the consumer at the seam, so nothing
        /// downstream re-checks; and nothing downstream may assume it is
        /// unclaimed, because the ceremony still drops it if another record
        /// already wears it.
        ///
        /// It is NEVER a key, never rendered in the visor's own voice, and
        /// appears in exactly one place: the naming ceremony's picker, first,
        /// foreign-attributed. The component is never told the outcome.
        ///
        /// ADDED AFTER WAVE ONE: the first cut of this record omitted it, so
        /// `marks.icon-offers` could take a nomination that no caller was able
        /// to supply — the mechanism built and unreachable. A record that
        /// cannot express its subject is the contract's bug, not the port's.
        nomination: option<string>,
        /// One line of visor-known metadata for the App settings sheet.
        /// `label` is THE VISOR'S word, never a component's; `value` may be
        /// component-influenced (a panel's declared destination), so the sheet
        /// renders it in APP VOICE. `foreign` says which.
        meta: option<surface-meta>,
        /// When the visor first assigned this record its mark. Shown as a
        /// date the user can check — "you have seen this before, since …".
        first-seen: option<u64>,
    }

    record surface-meta { label: string, value: string, foreign: bool }

    /// `VisorContext`. The bare kinds carry no surface because no component
    /// stands behind them.
    variant context {
        none,
        panel(surface),
        credentials(surface),
        naming(surface),
        storage(surface),
        settings,
        reset,
        events,
        device-picker,
        first-run,
        /// THE PAIRING CEREMONIES. Bare kinds for the same reason `settings`
        /// and `reset` are: no component stands behind either, and the strip's
        /// honest answer is the name of the ceremony.
        ///
        /// ADDED AFTER THE PAIRING WAVE, which had to borrow the context of
        /// whatever surface the ceremony was reached FROM — so the strip read
        /// "visor settings" while a comparison screen was up. Borrowing was
        /// the safe direction (it still reported a sheet as open, so the
        /// cluster stayed untappable) but it was not a true sentence, and the
        /// strip naming the sheet it is anchoring is the whole point of the
        /// two-line context.
        pairing-join,
        pairing-add,
    }

    /// The identity record: the user's own name, their word for this device,
    /// and the glyph on the identity circle. Shared because `embedder`'s
    /// commit notification carries it back to a consumer that mirrors it
    /// (the demo writes it through to the user-system partition).
    record identity { name: string, device: string, icon: string }

    /// `DrawerCloseOptions`, reduced to what the drawer host itself reads.
    /// The demo's custom keys (`commit`) were consumer state riding on the
    /// visor's option bag; a consumer can keep them on its own side.
    record close-reason {
        /// `context: false` — close without touching the strip's context.
        restore-context: bool,
    }
}

/// What the visor tells its embedder. The TypeScript `VisorHandlers` and the
/// `DrawerTenantSpec` lifecycle callbacks, as one-way notifications.
///
/// One-way on purpose: a callback that returned a value would be a synchronous
/// host call inside a guest activation, and the host's reentrancy gate
/// (host/src/dispatch.ts) exists because that is exactly what the component
/// model forbids.
interface embedder {
    use types.{surface, close-reason, identity};

    /// The strip's naming affordance was used ("name it", or the petname).
    request-naming: func(target: surface);
    /// The identity circle was pressed.
    request-settings: func();
    /// The back chevron was pressed.
    ///
    /// CONTRACT BUG, FOUND BY THE SPIKE: this was missing from the first cut,
    /// which left `#visor-back` rendered but inert — precisely what
    /// visor/ui/visor.ts:1294-1297 forbids ("an affordance that is present
    /// but inert teaches the user to distrust the ones that are present and
    /// live"), on the control visor.css:128-143 calls the unforgeable exit.
    /// The destination is the embedder's, exactly as `BackAction.on-back` is
    /// a consumer closure today; the guest owns only the chevron and the
    /// press.
    request-back: func();

    /// `DrawerTenantSpec.before-show`: the sheet is about to be revealed.
    /// The demo pauses its component runners here (input suspension).
    tenant-before-show: func(tenant: string);
    /// `before-collapse` / `after-collapse` / `after-restore`, in that order.
    tenant-before-collapse: func(tenant: string, reason: close-reason);
    tenant-after-collapse: func(tenant: string, reason: close-reason);
    tenant-after-restore: func(tenant: string, reason: close-reason);
    /// The arming delay elapsed; the sheet's controls are now live.
    tenant-armed: func(tenant: string);

    /// A sheet is wanted in the drawer: build it and hand its root to
    /// `control.mount-sheet`. The host cannot render into the guest's tree,
    /// so the guest asks and then waits for the mount.
    ///
    /// ONLY FOR TENANTS THE HOST OWNS. The visor's own ceremonies — naming,
    /// settings, reset, events — are rendered by the guest now, so they
    /// never emit this. A consumer's own sheets (the demo's credential sheet
    /// and storage picker) still do: the seam did not go away, it stopped
    /// being the only way a sheet can exist.
    tenant-build: func(tenant: string);
    /// The slide holding the foreign sheet is going away; drop the reference.
    tenant-unmount: func(tenant: string);

    // --- the ceremonies' consumer half (`VisorSheetsConfig`) -------------

    /// `canOpen` — asked before either ceremony opens; false refuses the
    /// open outright. This is where a consumer states a precedence its own
    /// tenants impose. Returns a value, unlike the notifications above: a
    /// guest calling out to a host import is an ordinary synchronous call
    /// (`store.get` already is one); what the component model forbids is the
    /// host re-entering the guest, which is why the *notifications* are
    /// one-way and these queries are not.
    can-open: func() -> bool;
    /// `beforeOpen` — run once `can-open` has agreed. The demo retires its
    /// panel and closes a modal dialog here, because a `<dialog>` paints in
    /// the TOP LAYER, above the pinned visor zone.
    before-open: func();

    /// `onNamed` — a petname and pet icon were committed for `provenance`.
    /// The table is already written; this is for the consumer's in-memory
    /// caches. `is-new` is deliberately absent: the ceremony IS the
    /// first-sight moment completing.
    on-named: func(provenance: string, petname: string, icon: string);
    /// `onForgotten` — the whole record for `provenance` was deleted.
    on-forgotten: func(provenance: string);
    /// `onIdentityCommitted` — the identity record and anchor hue were
    /// COMMITTED from the settings sheet (Save; never Cancel, never a live
    /// preview). Fires AFTER the visor's own write, so a consumer that fails
    /// cannot leave the visor's record half-committed.
    on-identity-committed: func(rec: identity, hue: u16);

    /// `nestedPlace` — while the consumer says it is showing a nested place
    /// with a live component surface on it, a ceremony BRACKETS it: the
    /// visor dims, and the consumer freezes the place itself. FREEZE IS NOT
    /// RETIREMENT — the component keeps its grants and loses only the user's
    /// input, because a ceremony is not a reason to destroy a session the
    /// user is in the middle of.
    nested-place-active: func() -> bool;
    nested-place-freeze: func();
    nested-place-thaw: func();

    /// `SheetAction.onSelect` — a consumer-contributed settings row was
    /// chosen, identified by its stable key. The consumer contributed a
    /// LABEL and this callback, never a node: the visor draws the button in
    /// its own chrome, so a consumer cannot paint on a sheet whose whole
    /// claim is that no one but the visor draws there.
    on-action: func(key: string);

    /// `onReset` — THE CONSUMER'S HALF OF THE ERASE, and the one import here
    /// that is both async and fallible.
    ///
    /// IT RUNS FIRST, BEFORE THE VISOR ERASES ANYTHING OF ITS OWN, and the
    /// ordering is the whole design (sheets.ts:445-462). This is the
    /// FALLIBLE half: on `err` the ceremony refuses with the reason line and
    /// NOTHING has been forgotten yet — the user is looking at a visor that
    /// still holds everything it held a second ago, which is a true sentence
    /// the sheet can say. Doing the infallible half first would buy a state
    /// nobody can describe.
    ///
    /// The exact inverse of `on-identity-committed`, deliberately: there the
    /// visor writes first and the consumer mirrors after, because a mirror
    /// can only be LATE, never contradictory. An erase inverts the risk — a
    /// late erase is a record that survived a wipe — so the rule inverts.
    ///
    /// Async because most of what a consumer drops lives in IndexedDB.
    on-reset: async func() -> result<_, string>;
}

/// The trust table (sheets.ts's `SurfaceMarks`), exported because it is
/// consumer-facing as well as ceremony-facing: the demo looks petnames up
/// per pane and renders from it.
///
/// EVERY FUNCTION IS STATELESS — each reads and writes the `marks` slot
/// afresh, holding nothing between calls. That is what made two facades over
/// one key the same table rather than two caches that can disagree, and it
/// survives the port unchanged.
interface marks {
    /// A pet icon from the curated table, or "" for UNMARKED. "" is a real,
    /// honest state, not a missing value: a record may have a first-sight
    /// timestamp and even a petname with no icon.
    record pet-mark { icon: string, first-seen: u64, petname: option<string> }
    record entry { provenance: string, mark: pet-mark }
    /// `is-new` is the first-sight moment: true exactly on the boot that
    /// created the record.
    record marked { mark: pet-mark, is-new: bool }
    /// `nominated` says the component asked to wear this glyph, so the sheet
    /// can attribute it to the component rather than to the visor. It is
    /// offered FIRST and only if genuinely free; a nomination for a glyph
    /// another record already wears is DROPPED SILENTLY, exactly like an
    /// invalid one, and the component learns nothing either way.
    record offer { glyph: string, nominated: bool }
    /// The colliding record — its petname as the user wrote it, and its
    /// unforgeable provenance key — so the visor can say what the clash is.
    record clash { key: string, petname: string }

    list-all: func() -> list<entry>;
    /// The record for this provenance, CREATING one — first-sight timestamp,
    /// and NO icon — if there is none. A fresh record is deliberately
    /// unmarked: a mark the user did not choose is a mark they cannot
    /// recognise, and the ceremony is where marks come from.
    mark: func(provenance: string) -> marked;
    /// `icon` must be a member of the curated table, or "" for unmarked;
    /// anything else is stored as "" rather than trusted.
    set-petname: func(provenance: string, petname: string, icon: string);
    /// Delete the WHOLE record. Forgetting must be honest: a component whose
    /// petname was dropped but whose mark survived would still be greeted as
    /// familiar.
    forget: func(provenance: string);
    /// Drop the whole table — the marks half of the erase, which must not
    /// have to enumerate records to be complete.
    erase-all: func();
    /// The pet icons no OTHER record is using; this record's own is included
    /// (re-picking what you already wear is not a collision).
    free-icons: func(provenance: string) -> list<string>;
    /// The ceremony's six offers, in render order. The non-nominated ones are
    /// drawn AT RANDOM from the free set, freshly per ceremony: a stable
    /// global ordering would mean every user sees the same first few glyphs,
    /// so an app's nomination would win by default-bias alone and a de-facto
    /// brand would form out of nothing but list order. Marks are the USER's
    /// vocabulary, not a namespace to be squatted.
    icon-offers: func(provenance: string, nomination: option<string>) -> list<offer>;
    /// Is this word already the user's name for a DIFFERENT component? Two
    /// records answering to one word would defeat the point of a petname.
    /// Compared trimmed and case-insensitively.
    collision: func(provenance: string, petname: string) -> option<clash>;
}

/// The visor's own ceremonies, which used to be TypeScript mounted into the
/// drawer as foreign DOM and are now rendered by the guest: the naming
/// ceremony, the settings sheet, the erase ceremony and the event list
/// (visor/ui/sheets.ts's `VisorSheets`).
///
/// The strip's own affordances reach these directly — `embedder.request-naming`
/// and `request-settings` are no longer round trips through the consumer for
/// these four. They stay exported because a consumer drives them too, and
/// because the e2e suite does.
interface sheets {
    use types.{surface, identity};

    /// One consumer-contributed action on the settings sheet — THE ONE
    /// EXTENSION POINT on a visor-owned sheet, and deliberately narrow. The
    /// label must be the CONSUMER'S OWN WORDS; a consumer that put a
    /// component-influenced string here would be lending the visor's voice,
    /// which the visor cannot check and the consumer must keep.
    record action {
        label: string,
        hint: option<string>,
        /// Stable key for `data-action`, for driving and tests. Also what
        /// `embedder.on-action` reports back.
        key: string,
    }

    /// The consumer's static contributions, set once at boot.
    ///
    /// `reset-consequences` are EXTRA LINES IN THE STATEMENT OF CONSEQUENCE
    /// — what else this consumer is about to destroy, which only it knows
    /// ("your saved storage provider", "the devices you paired"). Same
    /// discipline as `action.label`, and stricter in effect: these are the
    /// sentence the user is about to act on. An empty `extra-actions`
    /// renders NOTHING — no heading, no container, no separator.
    configure: func(reset-consequences: list<string>, extra-actions: list<action>);

    request-naming: func(target: surface);
    request-settings: func();
    /// Reachable in the UI ONLY from the settings sheet's danger entry.
    request-reset: func();
    /// Reachable in the UI ONLY from the settings sheet's "recent events"
    /// row — the badge that advertises it is a dot on the settings button,
    /// so the way in is through settings, exactly as the erase ceremony's is.
    request-events: func();

    close-naming: func(restore-context: bool);
    /// `commit` was a custom key on the old option bag; it is the settings
    /// sheet's own Save-versus-Cancel and belongs in the signature.
    close-settings: func(restore-context: bool, commit: bool);
    close-reset: func(restore-context: bool);
    close-events: func(restore-context: bool);

    naming-open: func() -> bool;
    settings-open: func() -> bool;
    reset-open: func() -> bool;
    events-open: func() -> bool;
}

/// The visor's API, as data.
///
/// Everything here is a method the TypeScript `Visor`/`DrawerHost` interface
/// has today (visor/ui/visor.ts:791-1209), minus the members whose type is
/// `HTMLElement`. Those are the interesting deletions and each is recorded at
/// its site.
interface control {
    use types.{surface, context, close-reason, identity};

    /// `BackAction`, minus the `on-back` closure: the guest renders the
    /// chevron and reports the press through `embedder`. `label` is the
    /// visor's own wording, used as title/aria-label and never as text.
    record back-action { label: option<string> }

    /// `DrawerTenantSpec`, minus the callbacks (they are `embedder`
    /// notifications) and minus `context`, which was a closure over the
    /// session and becomes an explicit argument to `open-tenant`.
    record tenant-spec {
        /// Diagnostic id; the key every other call names this tenant by.
        name: string,
        /// Framework vocabulary, fixed at registration — the noun phrase the
        /// drawer's spoken lifecycle sentence uses. Subject to the
        /// one-directional rule: never app-influenced.
        spoken: string,
        /// Refuses every other tenant's open while it holds the drawer.
        exclusive: bool,
        /// Holds the sheet's controls disabled for ARM-MS.
        armed: bool,
        /// Dims and freezes the page behind the sheet.
        dim: bool,
        /// Suspends (keeps its session, loses the drawer) instead of closing
        /// when another tenant opens over it.
        suspendable: bool,
    }

    record event-record { at: u64, text: string }

    // --- identity and the claim ---------------------------------------

    /// Roll the anchor colour and the audible anchor word, and let the
    /// cluster render. Returns whether this is a fresh identity.
    claim: func() -> bool;
    get-identity: func() -> identity;
    save-identity: func(rec: identity);

    /// The committed hue, for consumers that must paint with it.
    committed-hue: func() -> u16;
    /// Live preview (not persisted) / commit (painted and persisted).
    apply-hue: func(hue: u16);
    commit-hue: func(hue: u16);

    /// THERE IS NO GETTER FOR THE ANCHOR WORD, and that is the same
    /// structural decision the TypeScript interface makes (visor/README.md,
    /// "The channel decision"): the word must never reach pixels, so nothing
    /// may read it back. These two are the only doors, and both end in the
    /// visually-hidden live region.
    speak-word: func();
    reroll-word: func();

    // --- the strip ----------------------------------------------------

    set-context: func(ctx: context);
    /// Framework voice, flat string: it cannot carry class marking, so it
    /// cannot carry an app-influenced token. `ms` = 0 uses the default dwell.
    announce: func(text: string, ms: u32);
    pulse-context: func(sr-text: option<string>);
    set-back: func(action: option<back-action>);

    // --- the event record and standing conditions ---------------------

    add-event: func(text: string);
    list-events: func() -> list<event-record>;
    mark-events-seen: func();
    unseen-event-count: func() -> u32;
    /// Return value is the EDGE, so a poller announces once per crossing.
    set-condition: func(key: string, text: string) -> bool;
    clear-condition: func(key: string) -> bool;
    list-conditions: func() -> list<tuple<string, string>>;

    // --- the drawer host ----------------------------------------------

    /// Registration order is precedence order, exactly as the TypeScript
    /// drawer host's tenant array is.
    register-tenant: func(spec: tenant-spec);
    /// Opens (or re-presents) `tenant` with `ctx` on the strip. Returns false
    /// when an exclusive tenant refused it. The sheet itself arrives later:
    /// the guest emits `embedder.tenant-build` and waits for `mount-sheet`.
    open-tenant: func(tenant: string, ctx: context) -> bool;
    close-tenant: func(tenant: string, reason: close-reason);
    /// Re-present at a new size (same session, same sheet).
    rebuild-tenant: func(tenant: string);
    tenant-is-open: func(tenant: string) -> bool;
    tenant-is-suspended: func(tenant: string) -> bool;
    /// Recompute the strip's context from the tenant stack.
    restore-context: func();

    /// The host has appended the built sheet root into the live slide and
    /// measured it; `height` is its natural height in CSS pixels. This
    /// completes the `tenant-build` round trip and starts the reveal.
    mount-sheet: func(tenant: string, height: f64);
    /// The sheet's content changed size (the ResizeObserver the host keeps on
    /// the foreign root, and viewport resizes).
    resize-sheet: func(height: f64);

    /// Wipe every stored slot and return to the unclaimed grey dress.
    erase: func();
}

world visor {
    include polymorph:dioxus/app@0.6.0;

    import store;
    import chrome;
    import embedder;
    import pairing-driver;
    import entry-host;

    export control;
    export marks;
    export sheets;
    export pairing;
    export entry;
}

/// THE BACKEND CONTRACT FOR DEVICE ENROLLMENT (visor/ui/pairing-driver.ts).
///
/// A device joins an existing account, or an enrolled device admits a new one.
/// Both halves end in a SHORT AUTHENTICATION STRING the two humans compare out
/// of band: the point of the ceremony is that a relay which can see and modify
/// everything on the wire still cannot get a device enrolled without the two
/// people reading the same digits to each other. The visor's job is to render
/// those digits in visor pixels and to make the comparison an explicit human
/// act; everything cryptographic belongs to the backend.
///
/// Already async-and-fallible in TypeScript, which is why this interface is
/// nearly a transcription: every method there returns
/// `{ok: true, value} | {ok: false, error}`, i.e. exactly `result<T, string>`.
/// The visor is written against this and nothing wider — the demo satisfies it
/// twice, with a mock and with an engine adapter, and they are interchangeable.
interface pairing-driver {
    /// The join code and how long it stays valid. Rendered ONLY in visor
    /// pixels, as a QR and as its text form.
    record pair-offer { code: string, expires-ms: u64 }
    record pair-enrollment { user-group-id: string, partition-id: string }

    /// The joining device's view. `claimed` carries the digits to compare;
    /// `enrolled` is the ceremony completing.
    variant pair-join-state {
        waiting,
        claimed(string),
        confirmed-waiting,
        enrolled(pair-enrollment),
        expired,
        failed(string),
    }

    /// The admitting device's view. `sas-ready` carries the same digits the
    /// other side is showing.
    variant pair-add-state {
        connecting,
        sas-ready(string),
        waiting-peer,
        enrolled,
        failed(string),
    }

    /// The account profile, synced across the user's own devices.
    record us-profile { display-name: string, hue: u16, icon: option<list<u8>> }
    /// One trust-table row as the ACCOUNT holds it (distinct from the local
    /// `marks` table): the same subject, replicated between devices.
    record us-mark {
        provenance: string,
        petname: string,
        icon: string,
        nickname: option<string>,
        created-at: u64,
        /// The partition's conflict repair cleared one side; the user is asked
        /// to confirm rather than having a silent winner picked for them.
        needs-reconfirm: bool,
    }
    record us-device {
        agent-id: string,
        name: string,
        enrolled-at: u64,
        revoked: bool,
        endpoint: string,
        enrolled-by: string,
    }

    /// A change made on ANOTHER of the user's devices. Local-echo suppression
    /// is the backend's job, so the visor may announce every event it drains
    /// without second-guessing provenance — announced-never-silent.
    variant us-event {
        profile-changed,
        mark-added(string),
        mark-changed(string),
        /// provenance, then which field the repair cleared.
        mark-conflict-repaired(tuple<string, string>),
        device-added(string),
        device-revoked(string),
        /// The account's storage destination changed elsewhere. The payload is
        /// the ENGINE's own word for the provider — framework vocabulary, not
        /// app-influenced, so it is admissible inline in an announcement.
        /// Other devices ANNOUNCE such a change and never silently adopt it.
        storage-changed(string),
    }

    pair-join-start: async func() -> result<pair-offer, string>;
    pair-join-status: async func() -> result<pair-join-state, string>;
    /// The human said the digits matched. This is the whole ceremony.
    pair-join-confirm: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    pair-add-start: async func(code: string) -> result<_, string>;
    pair-add-status: async func() -> result<pair-add-state, string>;
    /// The human said the digits matched, and named the device being admitted.
    /// The name is TYPED BY THE USER and never prefilled.
    pair-add-confirm: async func(device-name: string) -> result<_, string>;
    pair-abort: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    us-profile-get: async func() -> result<us-profile, string>;
    us-marks-list: async func() -> result<list<us-mark>, string>;
    us-devices-list: async func() -> result<list<us-device>, string>;

    // WHAT THIS INTERFACE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY. The first cut
    // transcribed `PairingDriver` whole — nineteen functions — and the port
    // calls ten. The other nine are not the visor's: `user-create`,
    // `us-profile-set`, `us-mark-put`/`-forget`/`-confirm`,
    // `us-contacts-list`/`-put` and `us-device-revoke` are called by CONSUMER
    // surfaces (the demo's device management and contact list), which are not
    // in visor/ui/ and are not ported here.
    //
    // They are gone rather than kept-for-completeness. An import is a
    // capability the host must grant and a promise the guest may call; a
    // contract wider than its caller is a standing invitation to widen the
    // caller. If a consumer surface is ported later it should state the
    // functions it needs, at that point, with a caller to justify each.
    //
    // (`us-devices-list` stays despite having no caller in the Rust yet: the
    // TypeScript visor DOES call it — pairing.ts renders the account's device
    // list — so its absence from the port is a gap in the port, not evidence
    // that the visor does not need it.)

    /// Drain remotely-caused changes, for the announced-never-silent rule.
    us-events: async func() -> result<list<us-event>, string>;
}

/// THE ENTRY CEREMONIES' BACKEND (visor/ui/entry.ts): how a browser becomes a
/// device with an account. The picker is the one sheet that opens BEFORE the
/// visor has been claimed, so it renders on the unclaimed grey dress with
/// index content only — petnames and times, nothing personal.
interface entry-host {
    /// One row of the on-device index. `id` is opaque and never rendered.
    record picker-row {
        id: string,
        /// The user's own word for this device — the index rests in the clear
        /// and this is the one thing in it that is the user's vocabulary.
        petname: string,
        /// 0 = never opened.
        last-used: u64,
        /// The unseal policy, read BEFORE anything is attached, so the sheet
        /// can predict the ceremony. Mutually exclusive with `asks-passkey` by
        /// construction: a policy names exactly ONE ceremony to OFFER — and
        /// the offered one is not the only door, because the rungs are
        /// additive and a passkey row still reaches the passphrase field
        /// through the fallback control.
        asks-passphrase: bool,
        asks-passkey: bool,
    }

    /// A refusal that can land IN the sheet. `needs-passphrase` says the
    /// device is ASKING rather than FAILING, so the sheet reveals the field
    /// instead of merely reporting. A refusal that is not this shape is still
    /// reported — a backend failing unexpectedly must not leave the user
    /// staring at a dead sheet.
    record picker-refusal { needs-passphrase: bool, message: string }

    /// Open `row`, with the passphrase once its rung demanded one. `ok` = the
    /// device is open and the visor closes the sheet.
    open: async func(row: picker-row, passphrase: option<string>) -> result<_, picker-refusal>;
    /// Open `row` through its passkey ceremony. THE CEREMONY IS THE HOST'S,
    /// NOT THE VISOR'S: the host runs the platform assertion and derives the
    /// key that unwraps the device. `none` from `supports-passkey` means this
    /// page cannot, and the sheet says so rather than offering a dead control.
    open-with-passkey: async func(row: picker-row) -> result<_, picker-refusal>;
    supports-passkey: func() -> bool;
    /// Start a device that has no account yet.
    open-new: async func() -> result<_, string>;
    /// Recover an account onto this device. `supports-restore` false renders
    /// no control at all rather than an inert one.
    restore: async func() -> result<_, string>;
    supports-restore: func() -> bool;
    /// `FirstRunHost.newAccount` — the first-run fork's own half.
    new-account: async func() -> result<_, string>;
}

/// The pairing ceremonies, as the consumer drives them.
interface pairing {
    /// Join THIS device to an existing account: shows the code and the QR,
    /// then the digits to compare.
    request-join: func();
    /// Admit ANOTHER device to this account. Heavy ceremony: a statement of
    /// consequence, the drawer's arming delay, and a device name the user
    /// types that nothing ever prefills.
    request-add: func();
    join-open: func() -> bool;
    add-open: func() -> bool;
    close-pairing: func(restore-context: bool);
    /// Drain the account's remote-change events once and announce the
    /// difference. The consumer polls; the visor owns the words.
    drain-us-events: async func();

    /// Reconcile the boot cache against the partition and announce the
    /// difference — the other half of announced-never-silent, for changes that
    /// happened while this device was not running and so produced no event to
    /// drain. Called once at boot by the consumer.
    ///
    /// An EXPORT because the TypeScript's equivalent was simply called by the
    /// consumer; the port had the logic implemented and tested with no door to
    /// call it through, which is a contract omission rather than a port gap.
    reconcile: async func();
}

/// The entry ceremonies, as the consumer drives them.
interface entry {
    use entry-host.{picker-row};

    /// The device picker, on the unclaimed grey dress. `problem` seeds a
    /// refusal line for a consumer that already knows something went wrong.
    mount-device-picker: func(rows: list<picker-row>, problem: option<string>);
    /// The first-run fork: no account on this device yet.
    offer-first-run: func();
    picker-open: func() -> bool;
    first-run-open: func() -> bool;
}
